\b Topology:\b0\par
Firefox (LOOKUP) -> DBeaver (LOOKUP) -> Firefox (LOOKUP) -> Firefox (CREATE) -> DBeaver (LOOKUP) -> Visual Studio Code (LOOKUP) -> Visual Studio Code (CREATE) -> Visual Studio Code (MODIFY)\par\par
\b Request:\b0\par
As a data scientist supporting a cold-storage operations team, I will investigate disagreement between two colocated temperature sensors and assess whether a consistent clock offset explains their mismatched readings.\par
\par
In DBeaver, I will inspect synthetic sensor readings for duplicate timestamps, missing samples, sampling intervals and overlapping coverage. In Firefox, I will use a prepared local analysis dashboard to compare the sensor series at their original timestamps, then evaluate candidate clock offsets from minus ten to plus ten minutes in thirty-second increments.\par
\par
I will estimate the offset using the first three hours of readings, examining correlation, temperature differences and ambiguity between candidate offsets. I will then apply the selected offset unchanged to a separate final hour and assess whether agreement improves.\par
\par
In Visual Studio Code, I will save verification queries, inspect exported aligned readings and complete a diagnostic memo recommending whether a timestamp correction is supported, requires further investigation or should be rejected.\par\par
\b Task Description:\b0\par
1. In DBeaver, audit synthetic readings for source counts, duplicate timestamps, required fields, numeric validity, 30-second sampling, timestamp coverage and phase boundaries.\par
2. In Firefox, inspect original estimation-window temperature series and compare 41 candidate corrections from -600 to +600 seconds in 30-second increments using identical common-interior reference timestamps.\par
3. In Firefox, compare leading and competing estimation correlations, mean absolute errors and signed differences; select an offset using estimation evidence and inspect original versus proposed timestamp pairings.\par
4. In Firefox, freeze the selected offset before revealing validation and evaluate the unchanged correction on a separate final-hour window without cross-window pairs or retuning.\par
5. In Firefox, save candidate-offset scores, phase metrics, aligned pairs, a proposed corrected B series preserving original timestamps and temperatures, and an analysis summary; persist analysis tables for SQL verification.\par
6. In DBeaver, independently verify offset ranking using estimation-only SQL, reconstruct phase metrics from raw readings, check correction sign and window separation, and confirm exported pair integrity and unchanged source counts.\par
7. In Visual Studio Code, inspect exported CSV and JSON artifacts and retain the complete SQL verification script.\par
8. In Visual Studio Code, complete and review the diagnostic recommendation, distinguishing evidence for temporal alignment from residual temperature bias, calibration uncertainty, sensor-response alternatives and cold-storage safety conclusions.\par\par
